g reasoning is correct:\n\n> The notation $5 \\not\\mid 16$ means $5doesnotdivides//16$ It is equivalent to the statement that for $\\[6pt]$ $////forany$\n> $an//integer$ we have $16 = / = 5.k///$" 13194139533543 17592186045585 false] [17592186161727 1071 "Fill in the blanks so that the following reasoning is correct:\n\nThe notation $5 \\not\\mid 16$ means ![](images/fb/fb6fbc91b2218218acab5a0fe1baed797fc7ece0800c3280bc0d53af6d765ae3.svg) It is equivalent to the statement that for $\\[6pt]$ ![](images/48/48442365817dc8d3a14cc2f5931284b07e8cbfccf0bd2a097f7362f0f97348cb.svg)\n![](images/20/20865eee0320e3c8d4822421723a5a251048e7158888ac6416427f632bcb85b7.svg) we have ![](images/45/45453872ffd37b70f166312cf1aa4c3966437e91dc293ac0db198d895e41821c.svg)" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186161727 1074 "The notation $a \\not\\mid b$ means $a$ does not divide $b$. So, $5 \\not\\mid 16$ means $5$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ does not divide $b$ means that for any integer $k$, we have that\n\n$$\nb \\neq a k\n$$\n\nTherefore, $5 \\not\\mid 16$ is equivalent to the statement that for $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{any}}$ $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{integer}}$ $k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}16 \\neq 5k}\n$$" 13194139533543 17592186045585 false] [17592186161727 1074 "The notation $a \\not\\mid b$ means $a$ does not divide $b.$ So, $5 \\not\\mid 16$ means ![](images/83/833ccbc866a4951df8fa798f0780922c3dc56bff0b2baf1a36711c3f47c9f313.svg)\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ does not divide $b$ means that for any integer $k,$ we have that \n\n$$\nb \\neq a k.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $5 \\not\\mid 16$ is equivalent to the statement that for $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{ any}}$ $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{integer}}$ $k,$ we have $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}16 \\neq 5k}.$" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186161728 1071 "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y$?\n\n1. $4 \\mid (6x+9y)$\n2. $5 \\mid (6x+9y)$\n3. $3 \\mid (6x+9y)$" 13194139533543 17592186045585 false] [17592186161728 1071 "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $4 \\mid (6x+9y)$\n\n- $5 \\mid (6x+9y)$\n\n- $3 \\mid (6x+9y)$" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186161728 1074 "The notation $a \\mid b$ means $a$ divides $b$.\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that\n$b=a k$.\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1$, we have $6(0)+9(1) = 9$, which is not divisible by $4$.\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0$, we have $6(1)+9(0) = 6$, which is not divisible by $5$.\n- Statement III is true. Notice that\n$6x+9y = 3(2x+3y)$.\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $2x+3y$ must be an integer, too. So, $3 \\mid (6x+9y)$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533543 17592186045585 false] [17592186161728 1074 "The notation $a \\mid b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=a k.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $6(0)+9(1) = 9,$ which is not divisible by $4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $x=1$ and $y=0,$ we have $6(1)+9(0) = 6,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n6x+9y = 3(2x+3y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $2x+3y$ must be an integer, too. So, $3 \\mid (6x+9y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186161923 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407526 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186161923 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407534 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186161923 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407535 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186161923 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407536 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186161924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407525 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186161924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407527 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186161924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407528 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186161924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407529 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186161924 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407533 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186161925 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407530 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186161925 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407531 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186161925 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407532 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186161925 1038 #edb/ref 17592186407537 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186162213 1050 420.0 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186162470 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159980 13194139533543 17592186045585 false] [17592186162470 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407517 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186162470 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186162470 1071 "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y$?\n\n1. $6∣(42x + 24y)$\n2. $7∣(42x + 24y)$\n3. $2∣(42x + 24y)$" 13194139533543 17592186045585 false] [17592186162470 1071 "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\n- $6∣(42x+24y)$\n- $7∣(42x+24y)$\n- $2∣(42x+24y)$" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186162470 1074 "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n42x+24y=6(7x+4y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $7x+4y$ must be an integer, too. So, $6∣(42x+24y).$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, if $x=0$ and $y=1,$ we have $42(0)+24(1)=24,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n42x+24y=2(21x+12y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $21x+12y$ must be an integer, too. So, $2∣(42x+24y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186162471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159986 13194139533543 17592186045585 false] [17592186162471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159991 13194139533543 17592186045585 false] [17592186162471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159994 13194139533543 17592186045585 false] [17592186162471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407488 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186162471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407491 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186162471 1068 #edb/ref 17592186407497 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186162471 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186162471 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below." 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186162471 1071 "Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe notation $2∣8$ means {{field-1}}. It is equivalent to the statement that there exists {{field-2}} $k$ such that {{field-3}}." 13194139533543 17592186045585 false] [17592186162471 1074 "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$ So, $2∣8$ means $2\\text{ divides }8.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $2∣8$ is equivalent to the statement that there exists $\\text{an integer}$ $k$ such that $8=2k.$" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186163373 1064 "Suppose $a$ and $b$ are integers where\n\n$$\na\\neq 0\n$$\n\nWe say that $\\boldsymbol a$ **divides** $\\boldsymbol b$ and write\n$a\\mid b$\nif there exists an integer $k$ such that\n$b = ka$.\n\nWe can write this more formally as follows:\n\n$$\na\\mid b \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow\\qquad \\exists k \\in \\mathbb Z, b = ka\n$$\n\nFor example, $2\\mid 8$ because there exists an integer\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{k}} = {\\color{blue}{4}}\n$$\n\nsuch that\n\n$$\n8 = {\\color{blue}{4}}\\cdot 2\n$$" 13194139533543 17592186045585 false] [17592186163373 1064 "Suppose $a$ and $b$ are integers where $a\\neq 0.$ We say that $\\boldsymbol a$ **divides** $\\boldsymbol b$ and write \n\n$$\na\\mid b\n$$\n\nif there exists an integer $k$ such that\n\n$$\nb = ka.\n$$\n\nWe can write this more formally as follows:\n\n$$\na\\mid b \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow\\qquad \\exists k \\in \\mathbb Z, b = ka\n$$\n\nFor example, $2\\mid 8$ because there exists an integer ${\\color{blue}{k}} = {\\color{blue}{4}}$ such that\n\n$$\n8 = {\\color{blue}{4}}\\cdot 2.\n$$" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186163374 1064 "We have the following definition of integer divisibility.\n\n$$\na\\mid b \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow\\qquad \\exists k \\in \\mathbb Z, b = ka\n$$\n\nIf $a$ **does not divide** $b$, we write $a\\not\\mid b$. Therefore, by negating the two statements above, we get the following equivalence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}¬(a∣b) & ⇔¬(∃k ∈ Z, b = ka) \\\\ a∤b & ⇔∀k ∈ Z, b \\ne ka\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThus, $a\\not\\mid b$ means that\n\n$$\nb \\neq ak\n$$\n\n*for all* integers $k$.\n\nFor example, $3\\not \\mid 8$ because there does not exist an integer ${\\color{red}{k_2}}$ such that\n\n$$\n8 = {\\color{red}{k_2}}\\cdot 3\n$$" 13194139533543 17592186045585 false] [17592186163374 1064 "We have the following definition of integer divisibility. \n\n$$\na\\mid b \\qquad \\Leftrightarrow\\qquad \\exists k \\in \\mathbb Z, b = ka\n$$\n\nIf $a$ **does not divide** $b,$ we write $a\\not\\mid b.$ Therefore, by negating the two statements above, we get the following equivalence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(a∣b)\\, & ⇔\\,¬(∃k\\in Z,b=ka) \\\\ a∤b\\, & ⇔\\,∀k\\in Z,b\\ne ka\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $a\\not\\mid b$ means that $b \\neq ak$ *for all* integers $k.$\n\nFor example, $3\\not \\mid 8$ because there does not exist an integer ${\\color{red}{k_2}}$ such that\n\n$$\n8 = {\\color{red}{k_2}}\\cdot 3.\n$$" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407370 1081 "8=16k" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407370 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407370 1084 #uuid "0341c354-0b34-5253-835d-01bf5f74e9d3" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407371 1081 "an integer" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407371 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407371 1084 #uuid "046494f7-4180-59bc-84aa-9b1496ae4ecb" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407372 1081 "integer" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407372 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407372 1084 #uuid "0494a0c4-3cbc-5304-8920-956b6740222b" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407373 1081 "II and III only" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407373 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407373 1084 #uuid "070efb65-293c-5069-a098-e98293361bfb" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407374 1081 "II only" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407374 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407374 1084 #uuid "077fe147-bb8c-5245-adec-2f208cac3d00" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407375 1081 "number" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407375 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407375 1084 #uuid "0c0b9dc9-474b-5e1b-9fc4-be003d4dfe59" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407376 1081 "$16$ doesn't divide $4$" 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407376 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533543 17592186045585 true] [17592186407376 1084 #uuid "103cb213-0ae1-5f15-9c55